## 3\. Board & Topology

### 3.1 Hex Grid Specifications
| Parameter | Value | Rationale |
|----|----|----|
| **Shape** | Regular hexagonal region, radius R | Standard hex board |
| **Recommended R** | **7** (169 cells, 10.1% density) or **8** (217 cells, 7.8% density) | Sim-validated sweet spot: 8–14% ship-cell density, first-hit in 4–10 turns |
| **Orientation** | Flat-top | Matches existing prototype |
| **Coordinate system** | Axial (q, r) | Standard; `inBoard(q,r,R)` iff `max(|q|, |r|, |q+r|) ≤ R` |
| **Cell count formula** | `N = 3R² + 3R + 1` | R=7→169, R=8→217 |


### 3.2 Torus Wrap

Ships that exit one edge reappear on the opposite side. The hex torus uses **6 translation vectors**:

``` Y5Z363yidw
wrapOffsets(R) = [
  (-(2R+1),  R),  (2R+1, -R),
  (-(R+1),  -R),  (R+1,   R),
  ( R, -(2R+1)), (-R,  2R+1)
]
```


**Key simulation findings:**

* Wrap gives **statistically perfect occupancy uniformity** (cv = 0.003 at R=8) — no edge exploits

* Straight-line trajectories on the torus form **closed loops** whose length depends on heading:

  * Some headings (e.g., S at R=8) produce **Hamiltonian cycles** visiting all 217 cells
  * Others (e.g., SE at R=8) produce shorter sub-cycles (\~26 cells)
  * This is a **meaningful defender strategy axis** — heading choice determines cycle coverage

**Why wrap over reverse:**
| Criterion | Reverse | Wrap |
|----|----|----|
| Occupancy uniformity | Good (cv ≈ 0.05, mild center bias) | **Perfect** (cv ≈ 0.003) |
| Imperfect info | Good | **Excellent** (longer non-repeating cycles) |
| Intuitive (computer) | Good | **Equivalent** — renderer shows ships appearing on opposite edge |
| Spatial strategy | Has it (edge vs center) | None (homogeneous) — **this is a feature**: forces strategy into heading/speed, not position |
| Trajectory inference | Linear, bounded | Requires wrap-aware rendering — **trivially handled by renderer** |


### 3.3 Boundary Policy Comparison (Sim Results)
| Policy | Occupancy edge-ring rate | Verdict |
|----|----|----|
| Despawn | Ships exit in \~R+1 turns | ❌ Dead game |
| Reflect (naive) | Edge ring at **4.4× interior** | ❌ Broken — attacker shoots edges |
| Reverse (180°) | <sub>0.71× at edge,</sub> 1.1× at center | ✅ Viable but creates positional asymmetry |
| **Wrap (torus)** | **1.000 ± 0.001 everywhere** | ✅ **Selected** — perfect uniformity |


## 4\. Fleet & Movement Model

### 4.1 Fleet Composition
| Ship | ID | Size (cells) | HP | Base Cooldown | Speed Description |
|----|----|----|----|----|----|
| Battleship | BB-01 | 5 | 5 | 4 | Moves every 4th tick — lumbering |
| Cruiser | CA-02 | 4 | 4 | 3 | Moves every 3rd tick |
| Destroyer | DD-03 | 3 | 3 | 2 | Moves every 2nd tick |
| Destroyer | DD-04 | 3 | 3 | 2 | Moves every 2nd tick |
| PT Boat | PT-05 | 2 | 2 | 1 | Moves every tick — nimble |


**Cooldown formula:** `base_cooldown = max(1, size − 1)`

**Defender band (nice-to-have for v2):** Allow defender to set cooldown within ±1 of base during placement.

### 4.2 Movement Rules

Each ship is defined by the tuple: **(anchor_position, heading, cooldown, hp, since_last_move)**

**Per-tick movement logic:**

1. Increment `since_last_move` by 1

2. If `since_last_move ≥ cooldown`:

   * Attempt to advance 1 cell in the current heading direction
   * Apply wrap if the new position exits the board
   * Reset `since_last_move = 0`

4. Otherwise, the ship holds position

**Damage effect:** Each hit increments `cooldown` by +1. A PT boat (base cd 1) hit once becomes cd 2. A BB (base cd 4) hit twice becomes cd 6. This creates **compounding positive feedback** for the attacker: damaged ships are slower → easier to track → easier to hit again.

### 4.3 Ship Shape & Body

* A ship's body is a **rigid line of `size` contiguous hex cells** extending from the anchor along the heading direction
* The body does **not** shrink when hit (R16) — a damaged size-5 still occupies 5 cells
* The body persists at full size until HP reaches 0 (sunk), at which point all cells are removed

### 4.4 Collision / Avoidance (Turning Mechanics)

**Collision frequency is high** — simulations show **31% of turns** have at least one multi-ship cell overlap with random headings. Only 1% of 80-turn games have zero collisions. This is structural, not edge-case.

**Selected approach — Priority-based avoidance with turning:**

* **Priority:** Largest ships move first; they lock their cells; smaller ships must work around them

* **Pivot point:** Stern (cell 0 = anchor at q, r). When a ship turns, anchor stays fixed — only heading changes, swinging the bow to a new hex direction

* **Turn cadence:** Identical to movement — governed by `SHIP_SPEED.interval(size)`. A size-5 BB can only turn every 4th tick. A size-2 PT can turn every tick.

* **Decision AI per eligible ship each tick:**

  1. Try all 6 heading options in preference order: **straight → ±60° → ±120° → 180°**
  2. For each option, simulate move-forward-one and check cells against all locked cells
  3. Among collision-free options, **score by lookahead** (4 ticks of straight-line projection) — prefer the heading with the most open road ahead
  4. Ties broken by smallest turn angle
  5. If *every* option collides → **emergency stop** (⚠ blocked)

**Known issue:** After sufficient turns, ships may converge to parallel headings. A randomizer (periodic forced turn on larger ships) may be needed — flagged as open question.


## 5\. Probe & Damage Model

### 5.1 Attacker's Turn

1. **Click to aim** — click any hex to lock the aim (golden reticle). Click the aim cell again or press Escape to cancel.

2. **Click to fire** — with aim locked, click any *other* hex. Both cells are probed as a 2-cell salvo. As the mouse hovers, the candidate second cell previews in ghost-orange and the teletype updates live with the salvo label.

3. **Resolution** — each probed cell is evaluated independently:

   * **HIT**: a surviving ship has a body-segment at this cell on this turn. HP decrements by 1; cooldown increments by 1.
   * **MISS**: no ship body at this cell.

**Salvo labeling by hex distance** between the two cells: `dist 1 → TIGHT PAIR`, `dist 2 → WIDE SPREAD`, `dist ≥ 3 → RANGE N`. These labels are cosmetic vocabulary in the teletype — the player composes any 2-cell geometry directly, there are no preset patterns.

**Design note:** An earlier iteration used a radial picker with 4 preset spread patterns (tight/line/wide/wedge). It tested badly — the picker was too far from the click, had traverse-flash between buttons, fixed N-axis orientation, and zero on-board spatial feedback. Click-click exposes the full configuration space directly: every preset pattern is still achievable, but as a composed pair of clicks, not a button press.

### 5.2 Multi-Ship Cell Probes

If a world-cell has 2+ ships' body-segments, a probe results in a **single HIT** — the ship with the lowest body-index (closest to bow) on the highest-priority (largest) ship is hit. **The attacker sees only "HIT"** — no indication of multi-ship overlap.

### 5.3 Same-Cell-Hit-Twice

This is **structural, not edge-case**. A ship cycling on the torus will revisit the same world-cells after `(cycle_length − size) × cooldown` turns. Simulation at R=8 shows:

* Each cell on a ship's path is occupied for `size × cooldown` consecutive turns
* Then empty for `(cycle_len − size) × cooldown` turns (e.g., 44 turns for a size-4, cd-2 ship on a 26-step cycle)
* **17 out of 26** trajectory cells get multi-visited in an 80-turn game

The hit-count rule (R16) handles this cleanly: hit the same world cell 3 times on a size-3 ship = sunk, regardless of *where* on the body the probes landed.

### 5.4 Sinking

* **Condition:** HP reaches 0
* **Mid-game notification: NONE.** The attacker receives **no** on-sink notification — not which ship, not even *that* a ship was sunk. The only mid-game signal is the confirmed-sunk *count* shown in the Enemy Fleet header (e.g. "2/5 confirmed sunk"), derived from `fleet.filter(s → s.sunk).length`. The attacker sees the count; they never see *which* silhouette the count corresponds to.

**Design note:** An earlier design had configurable per-tier on-sink notifications (Classic: "Enemy destroyer sunk"; Standard: "Enemy vessel destroyed"; Hardcore: nothing). This was scrapped because any at-sink-tick reveal — even a generic "vessel destroyed" — tells the attacker *that their last salvo sank something*, which breaks the ambiguity preservation that is the entire point of R9/R18. If difficulty tiers are revisited, they would modulate the End-of-Game reveal detail, not mid-game notifications.

* **All reveal is End-of-Game only** (see §7.10). Trajectory animation, wreck placement, silhouette validation — all deferred to the EoG perspective. This is the **dopamine moment**, concentrated at game's end rather than distributed across sinks.
* **Mid-game hypothesis:** Because the attacker gets no sink confirmation, §7.5's Silhouette ↔ Hit Assignment mechanic lets the player commit attributions during play. EoG then validates those commitments against truth.


## 6\. Information Model & Inference

### 6.1 What the Attacker Knows

The attacker's **only** data source is a list of probe records:

``` bI7crikpG6
Probe = { turn: number, cell: {q, r}, result: HIT | MISS }
```

Everything else — heading, cooldown, ship identity, body-index — is hidden. The attacker infers trajectories by correlating timestamped hits.

### 6.2 Belief Pruning (Simulation Results)

From a prior space of 5,208 hypotheses (217 starts × 6 headings × 4 cooldowns):
| After... | Consistent hypotheses | % of prior |
|----|----|----|
| 1 hit | \~24 | 0.46% |
| **2 hits** | **\~1** | **0.02%** |
| 3 hits | 1 | 0.02% |


**Two hits with known timestamps are typically sufficient to uniquely identify a ship's trajectory** — heading, cooldown, and position are all determined. However, the player must correctly attribute both hits to the *same* ship — which is the core inferential challenge.

**⚠ Caveat — turning dynamics invalidate these numbers.** The belief-pruning table above assumes straight-line motion only. The collision-avoidance system (§4.4) introduces turning, which explodes the heading-per-segment search space — a ship that turned between two hits has far more feasible hypotheses than one on a fixed course. The current inference engine (as built) searches `(size ∈ {2,3,4,5}) × (heading 0..5) × (cooldown ∈ {1..5}) × (first-hit body-cell index)` ≈ 600 hypotheses per hit, **straight-line only**. Post-hit cooldown bumps and turning are not yet modeled. The 5,208 → \~24 → \~1 pruning cascade is therefore a *lower bound on ambiguity* — real games with turning will be harder to resolve from 2 hits alone.

### 6.3 Same-Cell Hit Pairs Are Highest-Information Events

The gap between two hits on the same cell constrains (heading, cooldown) tightly:

* Gap \< `size × cooldown`: same continuous occupancy window
* Gap ≈ `(cycle_len − size) × cooldown`: full wrap revisit — **pins heading and cooldown**
* Gap doesn't fit any legal (heading, cooldown): **two different ships**

The UI must make same-cell revisits **impossible to miss**.


## 7\. UX Design

### 7.1 Layout

* **Primary canvas**: Attacker board (hex grid with probe history) — 95% of screen time
* **Sidebar**: Fleet panels, inference aids, fire-order teletype, controls
* **Defender board**: Demoted to a **\~200px corner minimap** once placement locks; click-to-expand; pulses/flashes on incoming hits

### 7.2 Attacker Board — Cell Visual Lifecycle

All rendering stays **inside the hex's clip path** — nothing leaks outside the cell boundary.
| Age | Visual Treatment |
|----|----|
| **Fresh hit (this turn)** | Bright orange/red burn animation, bold turn-number badge, brief ship-fragment silhouette flash that fades to X |
| **Recent (last 3–5 turns)** | Full-saturation X, large turn label, slight glow if part of an active contact track |
| **Tracked (correlated to contact)** | Colored to that contact's track color, connected to siblings by faint vector arrow |
| **Stale (>10 turns)** | Desaturated X, smaller turn label — **never removed** |
| **Closed (ship sunk)** | Recolored to sunk-ship "case-closed" hue |
| **Miss (any age)** | Small grey dot, fading slightly over time, never disappearing |


**Multi-hit cells** show the most recent timestamp prominently with up to 3 older events stacked as small color-coded badges in the top-right corner; overflow shows `+N`. Hover reveals full history in sidebar popover.

**Concrete age boundaries (as built):** `Fresh = tick 0 (this turn)`, `Recent = 1–5`, `Aging = 6–10`, `Stale = >10`. These are discrete buckets, not a smooth gradient — the bucketed feel preserves cohort cues ("this hit is in the last-5-turns group"). Miss dots follow the same decay schedule (opacity floor 0.18, label opacity floor 0.30). All age boundaries derive from a single `AURA_FADE_TICKS` constant: `HIT_RECENT_THRU = AURA_FADE_TICKS`, `HIT_AGING_THRU = AURA_FADE_TICKS × 2`. Tune the one knob; hit buckets, miss opacity, and aura growth all track automatically.

**Attacker vs Defender rendering:** Attacker hit markers render at **probe location** (the cell the attacker clicked). Defender markers render at **body-cell index** (stern→bow damage visualization). The attacker never knew which body cell was hit — only where they probed. Defender markers are **not aged**; they visualize current damage state, not historical timing.

### 7.3 Hit Aura (Inference Aid — Toggle)

A single hit generates a faint red ring around the cell representing uncertainty. The ring **grows with elapsed turns** — the ship has had time to wander further. A fresh hit = tight circle. A 10-turn-old hit = huge soft cloud covering a third of the board. **The player sees their information decaying in real time.**

**Concrete formula (as built):**

* **Radius:** `auraR = hexR × max(1, 1 + age × 0.7)`, growth rate \~1 cell/tick, soft-capped at `state.radius` cells so old rings don't grow off-board.
* **Stroke:** `max(0.6, 2.0 − age × 0.08)` — fresh rings are crisp, old rings are thin.
* **Opacity:** `max(0.10, 0.32 − age × 0.012)` — natural "cloud" feel without a radial gradient.
* **Hits only, not misses.** A miss is *confirmed-empty* info — certain and negative. The aura models *uncertainty about current ship position*; a miss has nothing to do with that.

**Toggle:** Sidebar section "Inference Aids" between Enemy Fleet and Teletype. `state.showHitAura` (default `false`). Visibility is CSS-driven via `body[data-hit-aura="on/off"]` — the layer is always rendered, just hidden when off. No re-render needed on toggle.

**⚠ Unit gotcha:** `hexR` is the hex **circumradius**, NOT the cell-to-cell distance. Cell-to-cell distance = `√3 × hexR ≈ 1.73 × hexR`. Any length that expresses "N cells away" (aura radii, contact-track distances, trajectory spans) must use the cell-to-cell unit.

### 7.4 Contact Tracks

**AUTO mode only (v1).** Toggled via "Auto contact tracks" in the Inference Aids sidebar section. Visibility is CSS-driven via `body[data-contact-tracks]`. When toggled on, the engine recomputes; tracks are not maintained while the toggle is off (saves work in the common-off case). Reset alongside `declaredSunk` on lifecycle transitions.

**Inference engine (`inferTrack(hits)`).** Given a set of HIT probes, exhaustively searches `(size ∈ {2,3,4,5}) × (heading 0..5) × (cooldown ∈ {1..5}) × (first-hit body-cell index)` and returns every hypothesis whose trajectory places a body cell at every hit's (q,r) at every hit's tick. Torus wrap is folded in at the body-cell comparison step. **Straight-line motion only** — no turning, no post-hit cooldown bump (both deferred). \~600 hypotheses per hit, fast enough to recompute on every probe.

**AUTO correlation (`computeContactTracks()`).** Greedy single-pass: process hits in tick order; for each new hit, try adding it to each existing track and pick the track whose feasibility set survives best (most constrained = most informative). If no track stays feasible, start a new one. **Inference IS the correlation criterion** — no separate distance/time heuristic.

**Track confidence scoring** — four levels:
| Label | Meaning | Board visual |
|----|----|----|
| **1 HIT** | Single-hit track, \~420 hypotheses — no real attribution yet | Dashed line (trivially, just a point) |
| **CONSISTENT** | Exactly one hypothesis survives — heading/cooldown/size known | Solid line |
| **FORCED** | Multiple hypotheses survive but the set is constrained — UI shows ambiguity count | Dashed line |
| **INCONSISTENT** | No hypothesis survives — timestamps can't belong to one ship | Red + thicker line; glows red in sidebar |


**Sidebar Contacts panel.** Between Inference Aids and Teletype. One card per track with NATO phonetic name (Alpha/Bravo/Charlie…), hit count, inferred heading/cooldown/size (bold when narrowed to one, `N candidates` otherwise), confidence chip, and hit list (`T2 (3,5) → T7 (4,5)`). Empty state: `No contacts (toggle on to auto-correlate).`

**On-board lines** (`#layer-contacts`): thin colored segments between consecutive hits in each track. One color per track from `TRACK_COLORS`. Segments where `hexDistance > state.radius` are skipped — those almost certainly cross the torus wrap and a straight line would be misleading. Wrap-aware line rendering is deferred.

**MANUAL mode (deferred to v2):** The original design called for a dual AUTO/MANUAL toggle where hits drop as orphans and the player drag-selects or shift-clicks to form tracks, with an optional "💡 3 hits might be one ship — apply?" suggester. This was explicitly skipped alongside turning dynamics and wrap-aware lines (Stage 6b).

### 7.5 Enemy Fleet Silhouette Panel & Hit Assignment

Five ship silhouettes in the sidebar (5/4/3/3/2). Silhouettes show **sizes only**, never class names (R18). Player-side identity is "silhouette index" (0–4), not ship ID — the attacker has no truth mapping. `ENEMY_SILHOUETTES = [{idx:0, size:5}, {idx:1, size:4}, {idx:2, size:3}, {idx:3, size:3}, {idx:4, size:2}]`.

**Header tally:** `<N confirmed sunk> / 5 [· M hits · K declared]`. Confirmed count reads `fleet.filter(s → s.sunk).length` — just the count, never *which* (R18).

Each silhouette card shows: size label · inline SVG with N rounded-rect cell pips · `M of N assigned` · a small skull toggle for manual declared-sunk · status text.

**Silhouette states:**
| State | Visual |
|----|----|
| **At large** | Full ink silhouette, no marks |
| **Contact** | Silhouette + hit-count pip ("2/?") — requires hit→silhouette attribution via assignment |
| **Appears sunk** | Silhouette overlaid with ✕, dimmed, red left border — *player's hypothesis* via one of two paths (see below) |
| **Confirmed** | Wreck icon — End-of-Game only (§7.10) |


### 7.5.1 Silhouette ↔ Hit Assignment (Mid-Game Hypothesis Commitment)

**Why this exists:** Once sink-reveal moved to end-of-game only (§5.4), the silhouette panel was static decoration. Assignment gives it teeth — the player commits attribution mid-game by tagging X marks to silhouettes. EoG then validates those commitments against truth.

**Mechanic:**

* Click silhouette card body → toggle **assignment mode** for that silhouette. Card visually highlights with the silhouette's color.

* In assignment mode, click any hex on the board:

  * Hex has hit entries → toggle assignment of all hit entries at that cell to current silhouette.
  * Hex has no hits → exit assignment mode (click-away-to-deselect gesture).

* Escape → exit assignment mode.

* Click another silhouette → switch to its mode. Clicking an X tagged to silhouette A while in B's mode re-tags it to B. **Moving X's between ships is implicit** — no special UI.

* Multi-hit cells: all entries at that cell flip together (per-entry granularity deferred).

**Two paths to "appears sunk" — both alive in parallel:**
| Path | Trigger | Meta text |
|----|----|----|
| **Hunch** (manual) | Skull-toggle on the card — `declaredSunk` flag, hypothesis without evidence | DECLARED SUNK |
| **Evidence** (assignment) | `assignedCount ≥ size` — silhouette is "fulfilled" | ACCOUNTED FOR |
| **Both** | Manual flag AND fulfilled | DECLARED · ACCOUNTED FOR |


Removing an X drops below `size` and the auto-appears bit turns off — but a manual `declaredSunk` flag persists across that.

**Visual:** Tagged X marks render in the silhouette's color (overrides default red); age treatment opacity still applies. `SILHOUETTE_COLORS = [blue, teal, green, amber, purple]` — deliberately no red (would collide with unassigned X marks) and distinct from `TRACK_COLORS` (so auto-track clustering and player-committed attribution never collide visually).

**Mutual exclusion with firing:** Entering assignment mode clears any locked aim. Entering aim mode does not fire if assignment mode is active — the click-to-aim branch is gated. Modal because the player is doing two different things: firing vs analyzing.

**Lifecycle:** `attacker.assignmentMode` and per-entry tags clear on `resetSim` / `enterRunningMode` / `enterPlacementMode`.

