   * **Miss**: white-ink splash blooms, fades to grey dot.
   * **Hit**: orange/red flash, brief silhouette flicker, settles into persistent X.

6. **Teletype console** (bottom of sidebar, scrollable, \~4-line visible) narrates each salvo with timestamp and result, including explicit **REVISIT** callouts:

> `> SALVO 3 · T12 · TIGHT PAIR [· REVISIT] [· VESSEL DESTROYED]`
>
> `(3,0) HIT (last T=8)`
>
> `(3,1) MISS`

Salvos are grouped by `salvoId` in the display; data model stays flat (each probe is a single entry with a shared `salvoId`). Defender probes (from the Probe-toggle test tool) render as standalone `> PROBE · T#` blocks — no salvoId, visually distinct from attacker salvos.

### 7.8 Animation Language

All animations match the parchment/ink aesthetic:

* **Splash** = spreading wet circle that dries to a stain
* **Hit** = scorch mark that smolders for a turn then cools to ink
* **Sunk reveal** = ink line tracing the true trajectory across the board
* **Cell burn** = perfectly radial within the hex (no directional bias that would leak heading info)

### 7.9 Onboarding (Deferred)

* **Pre-game intro animation** (\~20 seconds): 3–4 ghost-ships looping the board under wrap so the player pattern-matches "ships wrap around"
* **First match is a tutorial** with slowed-down opponent ship and visible trajectory
* **Post-game replay**: shows each ship's actual path so the player retroactively evaluates their inference (now part of EoG flow, §7.10)

### 7.10 End-of-Game Perspective

**Architecture.** Three top-level perspectives: `Defender · Attacker · End of Game`. EoG is a third state — manually selectable via the perspective segmented control (auto-trigger when `fleet.filter(s → s.sunk).length === 5` is deferred). `body[data-perspective="endgame"]` hides all defender-only and attacker-only chrome.

**Sub-views.** A pill toggle inside the EoG pane: `FROM ATTACKER` vs `FROM DEFENDER`. Same underlying truth, different emphasis. State held in `state.endgameView`.
| Board element | FROM ATTACKER | FROM DEFENDER |
|----|----|----|
| Probe history (X marks, miss dots) | Full opacity, prominent | Dimmed (\~45%) |
| Ship hulls + trajectories + wrecks | Dimmed (\~50%) | Full opacity, prominent |
| Sidebar | Silhouette validation panel | Fleet outcome panel |


**Sidebar panels:**

* **FROM ATTACKER** — silhouette validation: for each silhouette (idx 0–4) shows actual size (now revealed), whether the player declared-sunk via assignment, whether it was actually sunk, and verdict: `✓` correct / `✗` false positive / `?` missed an actual sink. Score line at top.
* **FROM DEFENDER** — fleet outcome: per-ship card showing sunk-or-survived, hits taken, distance traveled (from `sim.history`), final heading.

**Interaction gating (load-bearing).** Every gameplay interaction is hard-disabled in EoG:

* Hex click → `probeCell`: gated by `state.perspective !== "endgame"`
* Silhouette card → `toggleAssignmentMode`: panel not rendered in EoG
* `setAim` / `fireDirectSalvo`: gated on `state.perspective === "attacker"`
* Spread picker, SHOT indicators, teletype line: all attacker-only, hidden by CSS
* Inference aid toggles (Hit aura, Contact tracks): UI hidden; state persists
* **Step Forward/Back: KEPT functional** — the user can scrub the sim and watch the EoG view update

**Deferred to Stage 7b:**

* Auto-trigger EoG when all 5 ships are sunk (currently manual via perspective button)
* Animated reveal sequence (trajectory line draw, sequential ship reveals)
* Wrap-aware trajectory polylines
* Click affordances inside EoG (hover wreck → see turn-of-sinking, click silhouette → truth highlight)


## 8\. Simulation Findings & Constants

### 8.1 Board Sizing
| R | Cells | Ship density | First-hit (turns, salvo=2) | Verdict |
|----|----|----|----|----|
| 5 | 91 | 18.7% | \~3 | Too cramped |
| 6 | 127 | 13.4% | \~4 | Borderline |
| **7** | **169** | **10.1%** | **\~5** | ✅ **Recommended** |
| **8** | **217** | **7.8%** | **\~6** | ✅ Alternate |
| 9 | 271 | 6.3% | \~7 | Borderline sparse |
| 10 | 331 | 5.1% | \~10 | Too sparse |


### 8.2 Cycle Lengths Under Wrap (R=8)
| Heading | Cycle length | % of board covered |
|----|----|----|
| S (0, +1) | **217** (Hamiltonian) | 100% |
| SE (+1, 0) | 26 | 12% |
| NE (+1, -1) | 25–42 (varies by start) | 12–19% |


**Design implication:** Heading choice is a meaningful defender strategy axis. Long-cycle headings give better board coverage but more predictable returns. Short-cycle headings are stealthier but revisit sooner.

### 8.3 Collision Frequency

With 5-ship fleet, random headings, R=8, cd=2:

* **31% of turns** have ≥1 collision (multi-ship cell overlap)
* Only **1% of games** have zero collisions across 80 turns
* With size-based cooldowns: rises to **34%**

### 8.4 Same-Cell Revisit Timing

For a size-4, cooldown-2 ship on a 26-step cycle at R=8:

* Occupancy window per cell: **8 consecutive turns** (`size × cooldown`)
* Gap between revisits: **44 turns** (`(cycle_len − size) × cooldown`)
* 17 of 26 trajectory cells get multi-visited in 80 turns


## 9\. Data Model

### 9.1 Ship State (Defender-Side, Full Truth)

```typescript UPjaJUQCTl
interface Ship {
  id: string;           // "BB-01", "DD-03", etc.
  size: number;         // 2–5, immutable
  hp: number;           // starts = size, decrements on hit, sunk at 0
  anchor: { q: number, r: number };  // stern position
  heading: number;      // index into HEX_DIRS (0–5)
  cooldown: number;     // base + hits_taken
  sinceMoved: number;   // ticks since last movement
}
```

### 9.2 Probe Record (Attacker-Side)

```typescript ESOa9G4Y97
interface ProbeRecord {
  turn: number;
  cell: { q: number, r: number };
  result: 'HIT' | 'MISS';
  salvoId?: number;                    // groups 2-cell salvos; absent for defender test probes
  attackerAssignedSilhouette?: number; // silhouette idx (0–4) if player tagged this hit
  // attacker does NOT know: shipId, bodyIndex
}
```

**A cell is not a state, it's a history.** The board accumulates probe records. The same cell can have many. Display is a function of the cell's full history.

### 9.3 Attacker Transient State

```typescript ri5qePVyVN
interface AttackerState {
  aim: { q: number, r: number } | null;      // locked aim cell
  hoverDestination: { q: number, r: number } | null;  // preview second cell
  assignmentMode: number | null;              // silhouette idx being assigned (0–4)
  declaredSunk: Set<number>;                  // silhouette indices manually declared sunk
  contacts: ContactTrack[];                   // auto-computed track list
}
```

**Separation from `state` and `sim`:** `state` is UI config (perspective, render mode, board params); `sim` is persistent simulation state (tick, history, hitLog); `attacker` is *transient interaction state* (aim, assignment, hover) that's meaningless outside the firing/analysis flow. Cleared on `resetSim` / `enterRunningMode` / `enterPlacementMode` / `setPerspective("defender")`.

### 9.4 Contact Track (Attacker-Side Hypothesis)

```typescript THmTh6DJnB
interface ContactTrack {
  id: string;           // "Alpha", "Bravo", etc. (NATO phonetic)
  hits: ProbeRecord[];  // attributed hits
  inferredHeading?: number;
  inferredCooldown?: number;
  inferredSize?: number;
  confidence: '1hit' | 'consistent' | 'forced' | 'inconsistent';
  feasibleCount: number; // number of surviving hypotheses
}
```

